{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And his brothers said to him, etc.</b> The reason for the repetition of <b>הֲמָלֹךְ אִם מָשׁוֹל</b> (\"Will you reign? Will you rule?\"): it is because there are two ways in the interpretation of the dream — one is actual kingship, and the second is dominion. Now, the kingship is in the category of doubt in the interpretation, but the dominion is in the category of certainty. Therefore they answered him both regarding the doubt of kingship and regarding the certainty of dominion. Regarding kingship they said ״הֲמָלֹךְ״ — do you imagine yourself to be king even in the category of doubt? And regarding the certainty of dominion they said ״אִם מָשׁוֹל״. And this is why Onkelos translated for kingship ״אַתְּ מְדַמֵּי״ and for dominion ״אַתְּ סְבִיר״. And the reason for the repetition of ״הֲמָלֹךְ תִּמְלֹךְ״ and that he did not suffice to say ״הֲתִמְלֹךְ עָלֵינוּ הֲתִמְשֹׁל בָּנוּ״ — they intended to express two astonishments: the first, regarding kingship in and of itself — even if he were not to be king except over a fraction of the peoples, it is a great thing; and this was not enough, but ״תִּמְלֹךְ עָלֵינוּ״ — for the tradition had already reached them that Judah would be king over all of them, so how could the vision come to contradict a true tradition? And also their saying ״הֲתִמְשֹׁל״ may be intended toward the matter itself." "<b>Further,</b> they may have intended to say in the repetition of the matter the following: since you are thinking of reigning, therefore the dream came that you will reign, etc., and likewise, will you rule, etc. — and the intention in this is ״רַעְיוֹנָךְ עַל מִשְׁכְּבָךְ סְלִיקוּ״ (Daniel 2:29)." "<b>And they added still, etc.</b> According to what I explained, that there was only one dream, the reason he said <b>עַל חֲלוֹמֹתָיו</b> in the plural is that within the dream there were many details: ״קָמָה אֲלֻמָּתִי״ one, and also ״נִצָּבָה״ two, and ״הִנֵּה תְסֻבֶּינָה״ etc.; therefore he used the plural." "<b>And on his words.</b> Meaning, that he spoke the dream to them — had he indeed dreamt it, he would not have uttered these words from his mouth:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what they said in the chapter הָרוֹאֶה (Berakhot 56a): Caesar said to Rabbi Yehoshua, etc., he thought about it all day, in the evening etc., ״רַעְיוֹנָךְ עַל מִשְׁכְּבָךְ״ etc. And therefore they suspected Joseph, for since he was thinking thoughts of domination over them, his imaginings, etc. And this is what is meant by <b>עַל דְּבָרָיו</b> — meaning, in the manner of the verse (Ecclesiastes 3:18) ״עַל דִּבְרַת בְּנֵי הָאָדָם״ etc., for through his deeds and thoughts in which he engaged in lording over the brothers, he brought about the dreaming of all these things." "<b>Or</b> it may say, in the manner of the saying in the chapter HaRo'eh (Berakhot 55b): ״כָּל חֶלְמָא דְּלָא…״ — like a letter that is not read; and this is its saying <b>concerning his dreams and concerning his words</b> — that he does not conceal them, and he seeks to fulfill them through what he tells, and seeks their interpretation. This proclaims that he desires to rule over his brothers, and this matter intensifies the hatred exceedingly." "<b>Or</b> it may mean <b>concerning his words</b> — that he speaks with them like a friend, like a brother, while his heart devises evil against them, and therefore the hatred grows great on account of this. Further, <b>concerning his words</b> — that he is not ashamed to speak to his brothers, who are older than him, face to face, that he will rule over them. This proclaims the haughtiness of his heart in speaking boldly of great things and thinking the matter is simple to the point that they would not take offense at it — and therefore their hatred grew, and what happened, happened."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:42.327884531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}